- Model: unsloth/Qwen3.6-35B-A3B-GGUF@a483e9e6, UD-Q4_K_XL, 22360456160 B (20.8 GiB), sha256 707a55a8… (= HF LFS oid). Device: 7.4 GiB shared DRAM, zram swap 3.8 GiB, NVMe.
- Run (UNSCORED — a C++ build was compiling concurrently): image jetson-c1-p03 (pin + patch 03, env unset = upstream), `llama-bench -lm mmap -ngl 99 -ncmoe 40 -p 64 -n 32 -r 1` → loads and runs: pp64 2.49 t/s, tg32 1.02 t/s, exit 0, wall 132 s (experiments/jetson/OM/feas-b1-a2). Earlier attempts: `-cmoe` not a llama-bench flag (recorded failure).
- Telemetry (cc/resource_observe io backend): NVMe read ≈ 52 GiB in 132 s (≈2.5x model size for 96 tokens), peak 825 MiB/s, device util 100%, ~41k read IOs/s → ≈20 KB per IO (page-fault granularity); pgmajfault ≤ 51k/s; workingset_refault_file ≤ 211k/s; zram swap in/out ≈ 50k/29k pages/s; MemAvailable min 2.7 GiB.
- Interpretation (hypothesis, to test): decode is NVMe access-pattern + cache-hit-rate bound: ~8 experts x 40 layers x ~2 MB ≈ 640 MB expert bytes/token vs ~0.8 GB/s fault-driven reads ≈ 1.3 t/s ceiling at near-zero hit rate. Levers: larger/aligned expert reads (direct-io, PR #31469), cache policy / routing-profile hot set (moe-cache, expert_replay), avoiding page-cache + device double residency, raising NVMe efficiency (fio ceiling unknown).
- Safety defect found and fixed: om_run passed `--kill-cmd "sg docker -c docker kill NAME"`; shlex split made sg run bare `docker` (help text) — a real overload would NOT have been stopped. Fixed quoting (`sg docker -c 'docker kill NAME'`); live forced-trip test killed only the owned container (exit 137, 0 containers left; experiments/jetson/OM/watchdog-killtest). Watchdog limits set to MemAvailable<600 MiB, zram>2.5 GiB, swap rate>150 MiB/s (the first run tripped on 33 MiB/s swap-rate — not dangerous; default 20 too tight for this workload).
- Also: telemetry records carry host_id from hostname ("satay") not campaign host id — fix later in resource_observe (minor).
# D030 — Over-memory baselines on Jetson; upstream moe-cache is a net loss on UMA (2026-11-10, jetson-arm)
Model Qwen3.6-35B-A3B UD-Q4_K_XL 20.8 GiB on 7.4 GiB shared DRAM (D029). All runs via cc/om_run/om_run.sh (watchdog + io/proc/tegrastats telemetry). Category: runtime-placement / cache-policy observations (no code change of ours yet).
## Baselines, pinned engine (jetson-c1-p03, env unset; llama-bench -p 128 -n 64 -r 2) — experiments/jetson/OM/base-20261110-0448
| config | pp t/s | tg t/s | outcome |
|---|---|---|---|
| -lm mmap -ngl 99 -ncmoe 40 (all experts via mmap/NVMe, rest GPU) | 4.49 | 1.30 | ok |
| -lm mmap -ngl 99 -ncmoe 38 (2 layers' experts on GPU) | 4.67 | 1.21 | ok (no gain) |
| -lm mmap -ngl 0 (CPU only) | 4.09 | 1.09 | ok |
| -lm none -ngl 99 -ncmoe 40 | — | — | watchdog: MemAvailable 401 MiB at 11 s |
| **default (-lm auto) -ngl 99 -ncmoe 40** | — | — | **watchdog: MemAvailable 321 MiB at 7 s** — default cannot run a >RAM model on Orin (auto disables mmap on iGPU) |
| repeat of row 1 | — | — | watchdog tripped on zram swap-rate burst 286 MiB/s with 3.5 GiB available (false positive → swap-rate limit raised to 1000 MiB/s; rerun queued in cmp sweep) |
Earlier 6 attempts failed on llama-bench flags (-c unsupported) — recorded.
## Upstream cf34d311 moe-cache (llama-completion, 800-char wiki prompt, -n 64, -lm mmap -ngl 99 -cmoe) — up-20261110-0436 (clean) + upv-20261110-0519 (with -lv 4 counters; TIMING CONTAMINATED: a CPU-heavy replay simulation ran at nice 0 for part of it)
| config | decode t/s (clean / upv) | decode hit rate | uploaded |
|---|---|---|---|
| no cache | 1.18, 1.19 / 1.21, 1.16 | — | — |
| cache 512 MiB | – / 0.98 | 37.7% | 23.0 GB |
| cache 1024 MiB | 0.91 / – | – | – |
| cache 2048 MiB | 0.98 / 0.98 | **64.0%** | 13.8 GB |
| 2048 + cpu-share 0.5 | 1.13 / – | – | – |
| 2048 + cpu-share 1.0 | – / 0.87 | 36.2% | 2.0 GB |
| 1024 + cpu-share 0.5 | – / 1.02 | 54.7% | 8.9 GB |
Interpretation: the cache achieves real hit rates (64% at 2 GiB ≈ 10% of experts — consistent with replay-sim LRU locality) yet decode is 17–23% slower than no cache. On UMA every miss still page-faults from NVMe AND is then copied into a separate device bank (~215 MB/token of copies at 2 GiB) while the bank's DRAM is subtracted from the page cache that serves misses. On discrete GPUs the upload buys PCIe-free GPU compute; on unified memory it is pure overhead + double residency. CPU-share (misses computed on CPU in place) recovers most of the loss but not more.
## Design consequence (hypothesis to test)
For unified-memory targets: do not copy experts into a separate cache. Keep each byte once (page cache / pinned host memory), let the GPU read hot experts in place (zero-copy mapped memory; Orin: pageableMemoryAccess=0, so pages must be registered/pinned), and spend DRAM on hit rate. Candidates: J-OM5 dedupe (release page-cache copy after upload) and a zero-copy hot set (J-OM6). Pinned vs upstream same-tool comparison pending (cmp sweep).
# D031 — Upstream llama.cpp +7% over-memory decode; NVMe is a 570 MiB/s budget drive; first-order decode model (2026-11-10, jetson-arm)
## Engine version (dependency update, not our code) — experiments/jetson/OM/cmp-20261110-0534
llama-bench -p 128 -n 64 -r 2 -lm mmap -ngl 99 -ncmoe 40, Qwen3.6-35B UD-Q4_K_XL, order pin,up,up,pin:
- decode: pin 1.255 / 1.284 (mean 1.27), upstream cf34d311 1.365 / 1.353 (mean 1.36) → **+7%**; prefill pin 4.52/4.24, up 4.77/4.75 → ≈+9%. Caveat: runs partly overlapped a nice-19 replay simulation; pin arms show larger spread. Category: engine-version change (upstream moe/mmq/graph work between Oct 18 and Nov 8), local to Jetson.
## NVMe ceiling (fio on the model file, O_DIRECT, read-only) — experiments/jetson/OM/fio
rand4k qd1 19.6 MiB/s (181 µs) · rand4k qd32 74 MiB/s · rand128k qd8 475 · rand576k qd1 416 · rand576k qd8 536 · rand2m qd4 543 · seq1m qd8 573 MiB/s · 576k psync×4 535.
PCIe link is Gen3 x4 (8 GT/s, x4 — /sys current_link_*), so the limit is the drive: "X15 Lite SSD 128GB" (budget, small capacity). max_hw_sectors 128 KiB, read_ahead 128 KiB. The 825 MiB/s peak seen in D029 telemetry is likely a sampling-interval artefact (to verify).
## First-order model (prediction tool, validated loosely)
decode_tps ≈ disk_BW / (routed_expert_bytes_per_token × miss_rate). With 615 MB/token (layout) and 570 MiB/s: miss_rate 1.0 → ~1.0 t/s; measured 1.27–1.36 → implied miss ≈ 0.70–0.75 (page cache acting as a ~30% hit cache). Replay LRU at 1300–2048 expert slots (2.5–3.9 GB): miss 0.38–0.27 → predicted 2.4–3.4 t/s if the cache holds bytes ONCE and misses read at full drive bandwidth. Our UMA components (dedupe / direct-fill) target exactly that gap. Belady bound at 2048: miss 0.12 → ~7.8 t/s (prefetch headroom).
## Consequences
- For the 128 GB unified + 4 TB SSD target, SSD read bandwidth and access granularity are first-order design inputs; record drive model + fio matrix in every host inventory (added to NEEDS-MAP).
- Page-fault reads (≈20 KB) vs expert-sized reads (576 KB qd8 = 536 MiB/s) matter less on this drive than on a fast NVMe (both near the ceiling); on fast drives large aligned reads should matter more — x86 to measure.
# D032 — UMA cache components A/B: dedupe works but does not help; direct-fill never activated; cache does not reduce disk reads (2026-11-10, jetson-arm)
Image campaign/llamacpp:jetson-up1108-mc = upstream cf34d311 + cc/moe_cache_profile/patches/combined-01-02-03 (Build CC 519 s). llama-completion p1 prompt, -n 64, -lm mmap -ngl 99 -cmoe -lv 4. experiments/jetson/OM/mc-20261110-0602.
| arm | decode t/s | cache hit | NVMe read GiB/run | refault GiB | min MemAvail MiB | output sha |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| base-a / base-b | 1.14 / 1.20 | – | 58.1 / 56.7 | 37.2 / 35.9 | 2936 / 3097 | 7a1541… |
| cache 2048 | 0.97 | 64.0% | 60.5 | 15.9 | 953 | dc4b37… |
| cache 2048 + uma-dedupe | 0.89 | 64.0% (22,437 madvise, 14.4 GB, 0 fail) | 61.4 | 14.0 | 1035 | dc4b37… |
| cache 2048 + direct-fill | 0.88 | (fill never used) | 62.7 | 16.4 | 950 | dc4b37… |
| cache 2048 + direct-fill + cpu-share 0.5 | 1.12 | – | 57.6 | 15.3 | 1059 | b7b6e9… |
| cache 1024 + direct-fill | 0.93 | – | – | – | – | dc4b37… |
Findings:
1. **The D030 double-residency hypothesis is not supported**: removing the page-cache copy (dedupe, verified 0 failures) made decode slower, not faster.
2. **The GPU cache does not reduce NVMe traffic at all** (57–63 GiB per run in every arm, ≈2.8x model per run); it only moves hits from page cache to the bank and adds copies. On UMA the page cache already IS the expert cache. GPU util 8–13%: decode is I/O bound.
3. Direct-fill (J-OM6) disabled itself: "not … expert-contiguous or not 4 KiB aligned; mmap fallback" — slices are 4 KiB multiples but the GGUF tensor base offset is (likely) only 32 B aligned; O_DIRECT needs aligned file offsets → fix = read an aligned window and copy the slice (J-OM6b, only if still useful).
4. Cache arms change generated text vs base (GPU computes cached experts, CPU computes the rest in base) → any cache result needs a KL check before counting.
New hypothesis H-RA (testing now): llama.cpp mmap setup issues FADV_SEQUENTIAL + MADV_WILLNEED over the whole file (llama-mmap.cpp:486-525; prefetch=-1 at llama-model-loader.cpp:1442) — on random expert access this means large readahead pollution; `--numa distribute` switches to prefetch=0 + MADV_RANDOM (zero-code test, sweep_numa.sh).
## Addendum: `--numa distribute` test was INVALID (switch inert)
numa-20261110-0622 (base, numa, numa, base): eval 1.17 / 1.19 / 1.20 / 1.22, NVMe 57.0/56.0/55.9/55.4 GiB, identical output. But `is_numa` comes from ggml_backend_cpu_is_numa → ggml_is_numa() = n_nodes > 1 (ggml-cpu.c:734): on single-node Orin the flag does not change the mmap advice. H-RA remains untested → real env-gated patch cc/mmap_advice/patches/01-mmap-advice-random.patch (LLAMA_MMAP_ADVICE=random), pipeline3. Lesson for methodology: verify a "zero-code switch" actually reaches the code path (log line or counter) before reading its A/B.
# D033 — Readahead wastes 57% of NVMe bytes but disabling it halves speed: the drive is IOPS-bound for small reads (2026-11-10, jetson-arm)
Patch cc/mmap_advice/patches/01-mmap-advice-random.patch (env LLAMA_MMAP_ADVICE=random: no FADV_SEQUENTIAL, no WILLNEED prefetch, MADV_RANDOM on the mapping), image jetson-up1108-ra (152 s build). llama-completion p1, -n 64, -lm mmap -ngl 99 -cmoe. experiments/jetson/OM/ra-20261110-0635, order base,rand,rand,base; switch verified active by log line (advice_log=1 only in rand arms).
| arm | prompt t/s | decode t/s | NVMe read/run | avg KiB per IO | output |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| base-a / base-b | 5.68 / 5.69 | 1.19 / 1.20 | 56.6 / 55.7 GiB | 93 / 95 | 7a1541… |
| random-a / random-b | 1.42 / 1.47 | 0.56 / 0.59 | 24.1 / 23.5 GiB | 4 / 4 | 7a1541… (identical) |
Interpretation:
- ~57% of the bytes the default reads are readahead the computation never uses ("H-RA" readahead pollution: confirmed as byte waste).
- But without readahead every fault is a 4 KiB random read; the drive does ~20 MiB/s at 4K qd1 (D031 fio) → speed halves. The waste was buying IO size. Both extremes are wrong.
- Target design (component J-OM7): MADV_RANDOM on expert tensors + router-informed exact prefetch: when a layer's top-k ids are known, madvise(MADV_WILLNEED) exactly those experts' gate/up/down byte ranges (≈1.9 MB/expert, contiguous slices) so the kernel issues large async reads for useful bytes only. Expected: ~24 GiB useful at expert-sized IO (fio 576K qd8 ≈ 536 MiB/s) ≈ 46 s IO vs 56 GiB at ~400 MiB/s ≈ 140 s → up to ~2x decode if IO dominates (prediction).
- Methodology: "zero-code switch" (--numa) was inert (D032 addendum); the real switch needed a log line to prove it applied.

