cc/memory_pareto/README.md:22:If the default CSV is absent, the script reads `lab/jobs/*/att-*/result.json`, the corresponding `job.json` (`spec.model`), and `lab/tests/*.json` (test-specific `model_file`, args and env). `--lab-root` overrides this fallback root. An explicitly supplied CSV path that is absent is an error; an invalid or empty existing CSV never triggers fallback.
cc/memory_pareto/README.md:24:Fallback accepts completed attempts and successful, nonpartial tests only. It emits a separate row per benchmark sample record or quality test; it never joins observations across tests, attempts, models or workloads. GPU MiB means `mem.gpu_peak_mib`, **not** baseline-subtracted delta; host MiB means `host_cgroup_peak_mib`. TG requires zero prompt tokens and positive generated tokens; PP requires zero generated tokens and positive prompt tokens. Combined PP+TG samples supply neither speed objective. `avg_ts` supplies speed; repeated `samples_ts` are not independently analyzed. Raw PPL and its stderr are recorded in notes, but no PPL ratio or KLD is invented. Fallback classification is explicitly `unclassified`, so these rows cannot support transferable claims. Job/test/sample identifiers and source result paths are retained. Attempts are not averaged or deduplicated.
cc/memory_pareto/README.md:38:This tool establishes **no statistical significance**, uncertainty bounds, speed stability, quality equivalence, or causal improvement. It uses exact input values without tolerance, confidence intervals or significance tests. A frontier is relative only to supplied observations and these objectives, not proof of general optimality. Host memory, PP, Top1 and PPL ratio are contextual columns, not optimization axes. Discrete-only rows remain labelled; neither they nor mixed/unclassified rows establish transferable savings. Fair claims require matched workloads, hardware, model/quality references and memory accounting, reviewed outside this script.
